 "new_string": "This confirms that the non-selected output must be inferred anew (it need not be the earlier demo's tile-background color), and that one-marker tiles do not qualify. Treat each recurrence as evidence for the exactly-two predicate while deriving marker, fallback color, separators, and tile geometry from current input/demo. Validate separator positions, tile shape, and row/column indexing carefully.\n\n### Additional accepted variant: task-3174de58 (15×15 demo; 8×5 test)\nA new recurrence instance reinforced the same tile-count rule with different dimensions and palette. In the 15×15 demo, full separator rows 3, 7, 11 and columns 3, 7, 11 (zero-based; color 7) divided the grid into 4×4 tiles of size 3×3. Marker color 6 on tile background 8 mapped to output 1 exactly for tiles containing two markers; other tiles mapped to 8. The demonstrated 4×4 output was `[[8,8,1,8],[8,8,1,8],[8,8,1,8],[8,1,8,1]]`.\n\nThe accepted 8×5 test had separator rows 2 and 5 and separator column 2, all color 4, giving 3×2 tiles of size 2×2. Marker 9 and background 2 yielded counts by tile `[[2,2],[1,1],[2,1]]`; therefore the accepted output was `[[1,1],[2,2],[1,2]]`. This further confirms: exactly two marker cells → 1, all other counts → that instance's tile background. Do not confuse separator color with either marker or fallback."
}

